    <h1>Bits and Bytes: Why Internet Speeds Look Different From File Sizes</h1>
    <p class="lead">Files are measured in bytes, but internet speeds are measured in bits. Eight bits make one byte. This guide explains why the two numbers look different, and how to compare them.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Two ways to measure data</h2>
<p>Files are measured in bytes, such as a 25 MB photo. Internet speed is measured in bits per second, such as 20 Mbps. A byte is made of eight bits, so the two numbers are not the same size.</p>
<h2>Why your download looks slower</h2>
<p>If your internet is 20 Mbps and the file is 25 MB, you might expect it to take a second or two. In fact, the file is 200 megabits, so it takes about 10 seconds at full speed.</p>
<h2>A simple rule</h2>
<p>To turn a speed in Mbps into megabytes per second, divide by eight. So 8 Mbps is about 1 MB per second.</p>
<h2>Why the sizes on a new device look smaller</h2>
<p>Some makers count 1,000 bytes as a kilobyte, others count 1,024. The gap grows with bigger sizes, so a drive may look a little smaller than the label says.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>Take a 25 MB photo and an internet speed of 20 Mbps (megabits per second). The photo is 25 × 8 = 200 megabits. At 20 megabits every second, it takes 200 ÷ 20 = 10 seconds.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Why does my download take longer than I expected?</summary><p>Speeds are in bits, and file sizes are in bytes. The download also shares its speed with other devices, so it can be slower still.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is 8 Mbps the same as 1 MB a second?</summary><p>Roughly, yes. Eight megabits make one megabyte.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What does a gigabit connection mean in everyday terms?</summary><p>A gigabit is 1,000 megabits. At best that is about 125 megabytes every second.</p></details>
      </section>
